--TEST--
`Core\Uri`'s seven readers, `with` and `toString` are one parse: over a table of absolute URIs every reader answers the component `toString` writes back, and `with` on one component leaves the other six exactly as they were — spec § 12, RFC 3986 § 5.3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Uri::host, Core\Uri::port, Core\Uri::path, Core\Uri::fragment, Core\Uri::toString, Core\Uri::with
// § 12: a `Uri` holds its whole text *and* its seven components, so the
// question this case asks is whether those two halves ever disagree. Every
// claim below is counted over the whole table rather than read off one line:
// a member that answers plausibly row by row still has to make the totals.
final class Read {
    // The seven components in RFC 3986 § 5.3's order, rendered so an absent
    // one (`null`) is not the empty string a present-but-empty one answers.
    // § 12's readers are `?string` for six of the seven — `path` is the
    // component every URI has — and that distinction is the whole point.
    public static function at(Core\Uri $u, int $i): string {
        if ($i == 0) {
            if ($u->scheme() == null) { return "-"; }
            return "=" . ($u->scheme() as string);
        }
        if ($i == 1) {
            if ($u->userInfo() == null) { return "-"; }
            return "=" . ($u->userInfo() as string);
        }
        if ($i == 2) {
            if ($u->host() == null) { return "-"; }
            return "=" . ($u->host() as string);
        }
        if ($i == 3) {
            if ($u->port() == null) { return "-"; }
            return "=" . ($u->port() as int);
        }
        if ($i == 4) { return "=" . $u->path(); }
        if ($i == 5) {
            if ($u->query() == null) { return "-"; }
            return "=" . ($u->query() as string);
        }
        if ($u->fragment() == null) { return "-"; }
        return "=" . ($u->fragment() as string);
    }

    // RFC 3986 § 5.3's recomposition, written out of the readers alone. It has
    // to equal `toString()`, which is the text `parse` was handed: that is the
    // sense in which the readers and the text are one parse rather than two.
    public static function text(Core\Uri $u): string {
        var $out = "";
        if ($u->scheme() != null) { $out = $out . ($u->scheme() as string) . ":"; }
        if ($u->host() != null) {
            $out = $out . "//";
            if ($u->userInfo() != null) { $out = $out . ($u->userInfo() as string) . "@"; }
            $out = $out . ($u->host() as string);
            if ($u->port() != null) { $out = $out . ":" . ($u->port() as int); }
        }
        $out = $out . $u->path();
        if ($u->query() != null) { $out = $out . "?" . ($u->query() as string); }
        if ($u->fragment() != null) { $out = $out . "#" . ($u->fragment() as string); }
        return $out;
    }

    // How many of the six components `$changed` is not carried through
    // untouched. `userInfo` is never `$changed` — it is the one component the
    // options bag does not carry, so `with` can neither add nor drop a
    // credential — which makes it part of every one of these six answers.
    public static function carried(Core\Uri $before, Core\Uri $after, int $changed): int {
        int $n = 0;
        int $i = 0;
        for ($i = 0; $i < 7; $i = $i + 1) {
            if ($i != $changed) {
                if (Read::at($after, $i) == Read::at($before, $i)) { $n = $n + 1; }
            }
        }
        return $n;
    }
}

// Absolute URIs, all with an authority, chosen so that between them every
// component is present in some row and absent in another: a mixed-case host,
// an empty path, a userinfo carrying a colon, an IPv6 literal with its
// brackets on, an empty query and an empty fragment.
array<string> $rows = [
    "https://alice@example.COM:8443/a/b%20c?x=1&y=2#top",
    "http://example.org/",
    "http://example.org",
    "ftp://user:pw@files.test:21/pub/a.txt",
    "http://[::1]:8080/x?q=%2F#f",
    "https://example.test/p?",
    "https://example.test/p#",
    "http://h/p?a=1#s",
];
int $trips = 0;
int $recomposed = 0;
int $carried = 0;
int $replaced = 0;
foreach ($rows as string $row) {
    var $u = Core\Uri::parse($row);
    // § 12: `parse` reports and does not normalize, so the text comes back
    // byte for byte — the default port still there, the host still in the
    // case it arrived in, the escapes still escaped.
    if ($u->toString() == $row) { $trips = $trips + 1; }
    if (Read::text($u) == $u->toString()) { $recomposed = $recomposed + 1; }

    // Six `with` calls per row, one per option the bag carries. Each asserts
    // three things: the other six components are unchanged, the named one is
    // the value asked for, and the result is still a URI whose readers
    // recompose to its own text.
    var $scheme = $u->with({scheme: "ftp"});
    $carried = $carried + Read::carried($u, $scheme, 0);
    if (Read::at($scheme, 0) == "=ftp") { $replaced = $replaced + 1; }
    if (Read::text($scheme) == $scheme->toString()) { $recomposed = $recomposed + 1; }

    var $host = $u->with({host: "other.test"});
    $carried = $carried + Read::carried($u, $host, 2);
    if (Read::at($host, 2) == "=other.test") { $replaced = $replaced + 1; }
    if (Read::text($host) == $host->toString()) { $recomposed = $recomposed + 1; }

    var $port = $u->with({port: 8080});
    $carried = $carried + Read::carried($u, $port, 3);
    if (Read::at($port, 3) == "=8080") { $replaced = $replaced + 1; }
    if (Read::text($port) == $port->toString()) { $recomposed = $recomposed + 1; }

    var $path = $u->with({path: "/new/path"});
    $carried = $carried + Read::carried($u, $path, 4);
    if (Read::at($path, 4) == "=/new/path") { $replaced = $replaced + 1; }
    if (Read::text($path) == $path->toString()) { $recomposed = $recomposed + 1; }

    var $query = $u->with({query: "z=9"});
    $carried = $carried + Read::carried($u, $query, 5);
    if (Read::at($query, 5) == "=z=9") { $replaced = $replaced + 1; }
    if (Read::text($query) == $query->toString()) { $recomposed = $recomposed + 1; }

    var $fragment = $u->with({fragment: "frag"});
    $carried = $carried + Read::carried($u, $fragment, 6);
    if (Read::at($fragment, 6) == "=frag") { $replaced = $replaced + 1; }
    if (Read::text($fragment) == $fragment->toString()) { $recomposed = $recomposed + 1; }
}
// 8 parses, and 48 `Uri`s built by `with` on top of them: 56 recompositions,
// 288 components carried across a replacement, 48 components replaced.
echo "round trips ", $trips, "/8\n";
echo "recomposed ", $recomposed, "/56\n";
echo "carried ", $carried, "/288\n";
echo "replaced ", $replaced, "/48\n";

// The one URI in RFC 3986's grammar for which the sweep's invariant does not
// hold, asserted on both sides. A port component written empty is not a port
// (§ 3.2.3), so `port()` is `null` and the readers recompose to text without
// the `:` — while `toString()` still reports what was written, because `parse`
// reports. `with` is where the difference becomes visible: it recomposes, so
// the `:` a caller never mentioned disappears. `nvs_core_uri_with`'s own doc
// comment names this as the one place a round trip through `with` is not the
// identity; a port that is actually there survives untouched.
var $empty = Core\Uri::parse("http://h:/p");
echo $empty->toString(), " ", Read::at($empty, 3), " ", Read::text($empty), "\n";
echo $empty->with({query: "a"})->toString(), "\n";
var $held = Core\Uri::parse("http://h:8080/p");
echo $held->with({query: "a"})->toString(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
round trips 8/8
recomposed 56/56
carried 288/288
replaced 48/48
http://h:/p - http://h/p
http://h/p?a
http://h:8080/p?a
